# Source files are normalised to LF in the repository, so a diff reads the same
# on every machine and nobody sees a whole file rewritten because of a checkout.
* text=auto

# Fixtures are the exception, and the reason this file exists.
#
# A fixture is a copy of the bytes an authority's server actually sent. Turkish
# public sites are largely IIS and send CRLF, and with normalisation on, git was
# storing 126 263 bytes for a page the server sent as 129 075 - 2 812 line
# endings quietly rewritten. On Windows the checkout converted them back, so
# nothing looked wrong; on the Linux machine this is meant to run on (D-10) the
# fixture would have differed from the captured page by every line.
#
# D-09 says a fixture must be a real captured page, and D-11 says the archive
# holds bytes rather than decoded text, for the same reason: what gets modified
# on the way in cannot be recovered by reprocessing later. A fixture git has
# edited is no longer evidence of what the site returned.
tests/ProcurementRadar.Parsers.Tests/fixtures/** -text

# Archived bodies, if one ever ends up tracked. Compressed data must not be
# touched at all.
*.gz -text
*.zip -text
*.pdf -text

# Saved third-party web pages used only as parser fixtures; keep them out of language stats.
tests/**/fixtures/** linguist-vendored
docs/measurements/** linguist-vendored
